1831			lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10, lem:qld-comm-cons,
1832			def:ith-restricted-line}
1833		By Cauchy--Schwarz it suffices to bound the deficit
1834		$1 - \E \sum_b \bra{\hat\psi} T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z,z}_{b} \ot
1835		\hat{M}^{(\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \ket{\hat\psi}$,
1836		where
1837		$T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z,z}_{b}
1838		= \sum_{f_X} T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z}_{f_X,[\mathrm{eval}_z(\cdot)=b]}$.
1839		By Property~2 of Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-sublines}, the expectation can be
1840		rewritten over the mixture of product distributions
1841		$D_{\mathrm{v},i} \times D_{\mathrm{v},j}$ with $z$ uniformly random on
1842		$\ell_Z$, and, since
1843		$T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z,z}_{b}
1844		= \E_{x \in \ell_X} \sum_a
1845		T^{\ell_X,\ell_Z}
1846		_{[\mathrm{eval}_x(\cdot)=a,\ \mathrm{eval}_z(\cdot)=b]}$,
1847		one may additionally average over $x$ uniformly random on $\ell_X$. Up
1848		to error $m(\sqrt{\delta_P} + \sqrt{\delta_Q})$ --- obtained by
1849		applying the error inflation of
1850		Lemma~\ref{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds} to the conclusion of
1851		Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} (transported to the sub-line distribution
1852		via Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-sublines}) and to the consistency relations of
1853		Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-4-10} --- the resulting quantity is close to
1854		$\E \sum_{a,b} \bra{\hat\psi}
1855		\hat{M}^{(\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}
1856		\hat{M}^{(\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \ot
1857		\hat{M}^{(\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \ket{\hat\psi}$,
1858		which, since $\{\hat{M}^{(\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\}$ is a measurement,
1859		equals
1860		$\sum_b \bra{\hat\psi} \hat{M}^{(\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \ot
1861		\hat{M}^{(\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \ket{\hat\psi}
1862		\ge 1 - \sqrt{\eps}$
1863		by the self-consistency of the points measurements
1864		(Lemma~\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}). The deficit is therefore
1865		$O(m(\sqrt{\delta_P} + \sqrt{\delta_Q}) + \sqrt{\eps})$, and taking the
1866		square root gives the claim.
